,"g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":37.946059,"lo":23.643354,"d":591},{"n":"Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":37.942706,"lo":23.645687,"d":591},{"n":"Χαραλαμπάκης Ιωάννης","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":37.936633,"lo":23.639415,"d":597},{"n":"National Bank of Greece","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":37.945012,"lo":23.644949,"d":619,"ph":"2104144221"},{"n":"Χατζηκλημάνογλου Συμεών","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":37.945424,"lo":23.644632,"d":623},{"n":"Π. Κρεββατάς","g":"essentials","k":"dry_cleaning","la":37.936457,"lo":23.64022,"d":625},{"n":"Eurobank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":37.945737,"lo":23.644429,"d":631},{"n":"Sklavenitis","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":37.936269,"lo":23.638723,"d":637,"h":"Mo-Su 08:00-21:00"},{"n":"Κουτρούλη Στυλιανή","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":37.94014,"lo":23.645893,"d":638},{"n":"Διαχρονικό","g":"essentials","k":"internet_cafe","la":37.941205,"lo":23.64623,"d":640,"h":"Mo-Sa 09:00-00:00","ph":"2104297731"},{"n":"Eurobank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":37.936398,"lo":23.64139,"d":657},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":37.947391,"lo":23.642496,"d":673,"w":"https://www.nbg.gr/"},{"n":"Πλακας","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":37.936185,"lo":23.641201,"d":674},{"n":"Piraeus","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.943,"lo":23.646944,"d":705,"ds":"Piraeus ( py-REE-əs, pirr-AY-əs; Greek: Πειραιάς Peiraiás [pireˈas]; Ancient Greek and Katharevousa: Πειραιεύς Peiraieús, Ancient: [peːrai̯eús], Katharevousa: [pireˈefs]) is a port city within the Athens urban area (\"Greater Athens\"), in the Attica region of Greece. It is located eight kilometres (5 mi) southwest of Athens city centre along the east coast of the Saronic Gulf in the Athens Riviera.","wpu":"Piraeus","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/2011_Dimos_Pireos.png/960px-2011_Dimos_Pireos.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Archaeological Museum of Piraeus","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.937222,"lo":23.644444,"d":714,"ds":"The Archaeological Museum of Piraeus is a museum in Piraeus, a port city within the Athens urban area in Greece. It contains mainly sculptures, discovered in Piraeus and in the area of the Attic coast from Bronze Age to Roman times.","wpu":"Archaeological Museum of Piraeus","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/7651_-_Piraeus_Arch._Museum%2C_Athens_-_Greek_theatre_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_14_2009.jpg/960px-7651_-_Piraeus_Arch._Museum%2C_Athens_-_Greek_theatre_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_14_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Electric Railways Museum of Piraeus","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.948139,"lo":23.642611,"d":752,"ds":"The Electric Railways Museum of Piraeus (Greek: Μουσείο Ηλεκτρικών Σιδηροδρόμων) is a railway museum in Piraeus, Athens, Greece. The museum opened in 2005, displaying the personal collection of a former railway employee.","wpu":"Electric Railways Museum of Piraeus","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Piraeus_Station_Electric_railways_museum.jpg/960px-Piraeus_Station_Electric_railways_museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bay of Zea","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.9375,"lo":23.648611,"d":980,"ds":"The Bay of Zea (Greek: Λιμένας Ζέας, romanized: Limenas Zeas), since Ottoman times and until recently known as Paşalimanı (Πασαλιμάνι), is a broad bay located at the eastern coast of the Piraeus peninsula in Attica, Greece. It hosted the swimming events at the 1896 Summer Olympics held in Attica. A seaport and marina are in the bay.","wpu":"Bay of Zea","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Piraeus_harbor_3-2004.JPG/960px-Piraeus_harbor_3-2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hellenic Maritime Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.9325,"lo":23.645917,"d":1218,"ds":"The first attempt to establish the Hellenic Maritime Museum was in the newly established Greek state in 1867. That year the master of the Navy Gerasimos Zochios, founder of the Navy Retirement Fund, suggested that the Fund takes the task of collecting and conserving objects related to the Greek maritime history.","wpu":"Hellenic Maritime Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Hellenic_Maritime_Museum_frontyard.jpg/960px-Hellenic_Maritime_Museum_frontyard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Veakeio Municipal Theater","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.9425,"lo":23.6533,"d":1255,"ds":"The Veakio Municipal Theater (Greek: Βεάκειο Δημοτικό Θέατρο) is a 2,000-seat open-air amphitheater located in the Kastella neighborhood of Piraeus, Greece. Built in 1969 during Greece's military junta period, the theater was originally named \"Skylitsio\" after the junta-appointed mayor who commissioned it.","wpu":"Veakeio Municipal Theater","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Veakio_Theater_%2801%29.jpg/960px-Veakio_Theater_%2801%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Greek Genocide Memorial in Piraeus","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.93416,"lo":23.65024,"d":1315,"ds":"The Greek genocide Memorial in Piraeus is a major monument located in the Greek port city of Piraeus. It commemorates the genocide of the Pontic Greeks. The monument is situated in the Alexandra Square in Piraeus.","wpu":"Greek Genocide Memorial in Piraeus","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Alexandra%27s_Square.jpg/960px-Alexandra%27s_Square.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Psyttaleia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.94,"lo":23.588,"d":4477,"ds":"Psyttaleia (Greek: Ψυττάλεια) is an uninhabited island in the Saronic Gulf between the harbor of Piraeus and the Kynosoura peninsula on Salamis Island, Greece. It covers an area of 0.375 square kilometers. The island houses Athens' largest sewage treatment plant, which is also the largest in Europe, with a projected daily maximum drying capacity of 750 tons of sewage.","wpu":"Psyttaleia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Psyttaleia.jpg/960px-Psyttaleia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Tactual Museum of Athens","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.94548,"lo":23.70003,"d":5365,"ds":"The Tactual Museum of Athens is a museum for the visually impaired in Kallithea, Athens, Greece. It was founded in 1984 by the Kallithea-based association Lighthouse for the Blind of Greece to allow visually impaired people to become familiarised with the cultural heritage of Greece. The museum exhibits copies of original artifacts displayed in other Greek museums that may be touched by all visitors.","wpu":"Tactual Museum of Athens","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Tactual_Museum_of_Athens.jpg/960px-Tactual_Museum_of_Athens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Atalanti Island (Attica)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.935,"lo":23.5725,"d":5883,"ds":"Atalanti Island (Greek: Νησίδα Αταλάντη, romanized: Nisída Atalánti) is an island off the western coast of Attica region, between Salamis Island and the port of Athens, Piraeus. Anciently, the island was called Atalanta or Atalante (Αταλάντη).","wpu":"Atalanti Island (Attica)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Psyttaleia.jpg/960px-Psyttaleia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Athens is the capital and largest city of Greece. A significant coastal urban area in the Mediterranean, Athens is also the capital of the Attica region and is the southernmost capital on the European mainland. With its urban area's population numbering over 3.6 million, it is the eighth-largest urban area in the European Union (EU).","wpu":"Athens","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg/960px-Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}